00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:21.720
There was a time, earlier in 2020, when there
was no news but COVID news.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:27.740
There was this time when every word of every
story of every front-page of every paper either

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:35.420
directly or indirectly dealt with the Coronavirus,
but, now, many months on, that is no longer

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:36.800
the case.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.149
The world has awoken in a way that there is
more of relevance.

00:00:41.149 --> 00:00:49.249
However, in certain circles, in those most
dramatically impacted, COVID is still everything.

00:00:49.249 --> 00:00:52.239
That is certainly the case in aviation.

00:00:52.239 --> 00:00:57.690
Almost nothing they do nowadays is not dictated
by the ever-present backdrop of the century’s

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.449
first pandemic.

00:00:59.449 --> 00:01:03.579
Most of what anyone has been talking about
recently, including us, has been on how this

00:01:03.579 --> 00:01:08.790
will fundamentally change the industry—on
how the airline of 2029 will look markedly

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:10.340
different than that of 2019.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:17.220
Today, though, in October, 2020, we’re nearing
the one-year mark since when SARS-CoV-2 is

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.020
believed to have made the jump from animal
to human.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:25.190
With this amount of time behind us, the airline
industry has already changed.

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:31.290
The airline of October, 2020 is already different
than the airline of October, 2019.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:36.640
So, to see what’s happened already, we’re
going to look at eight flights that, for reasons

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.950
either big or small, would have been considered
strange, unique, or even unimaginable just

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:46.670
a year ago today, but now, are nothing out
of the ordinary.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:51.810
We’ll start with the end for one aircraft—at
least for a while.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:57.820
On September 25th, Qantas’ VH-OQI, an a380,
flew from Dresden, Germany to Victorville,

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:03.170
California—a route that is quite clearly
unordinary for an airline based in Australia,

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:06.180
but its not the route that made this notable.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:10.249
VH-OQI had been flown to Dresden in March
for cabin refurbishment.

00:02:10.249 --> 00:02:15.930
By September, though, it became clear that
not only was the refurbishment unnecessary,

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:18.319
but that the aircraft was too.

00:02:27.133 --> 00:02:30.974
airborne until, at least, 2023.

00:02:30.974 --> 00:02:35.974
This demonstrates that this is the soonest
Qantas believes passenger demand could return

00:02:35.974 --> 00:02:40.523
to 2019 levels—when the a380 served as its
flagship aircraft.

00:02:40.523 --> 00:02:45.444
However, the fact, specifically, that the
airline would bother storing the aircraft,

00:02:45.444 --> 00:02:50.474
rather than scrapping or selling it, also
marks a hint of optimism.

00:02:50.474 --> 00:02:55.453
Long-term aircraft storage is not cheap—it
involves quite regular maintenance and monitoring—and

00:02:55.453 --> 00:03:01.314
so this means that Qantas does truly believe
that demand will return to the levels of before—an

00:03:01.314 --> 00:03:03.483
opinion not held by all.

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:07.413
Qantas desperately needs every dollar they
can get right now, so they would only spend

00:03:07.413 --> 00:03:11.754
money on keeping their a380s if they truly
believed that there was a significant need

00:03:11.754 --> 00:03:14.993
for super-large aircraft in the medium-term
future.

00:03:14.993 --> 00:03:19.924
Therefore, while its plenty easy to find negative
signs for the future of aviation, this flight

00:03:19.924 --> 00:03:24.733
does represent a belief that the good times
will return for airlines.

00:03:24.733 --> 00:03:29.683
This next flight, meanwhile, didn’t, and,
in some ways, couldn’t exist a year ago.

00:03:29.683 --> 00:03:35.554
On October 8th, Southwest flight 1920 flew
from Phoenix, Arizona to San Jose del Cabo,

00:03:35.554 --> 00:03:36.554
Mexico.

00:03:36.554 --> 00:03:40.694
This certainly looks like a fairly normal
flight—just another big city to resort town

00:03:40.694 --> 00:03:44.864
route—but the backstory behind it explains
its peculiarity.

00:03:44.864 --> 00:03:50.403
Mexico is one of the few major countries that
has not restricted entry to Americans, so,

00:03:50.403 --> 00:03:55.574
when the US emerged from lockdown in May and
June, Cabo was one of the first international

00:03:55.574 --> 00:03:58.634
destinations added back to Southwest’s network.

00:03:58.634 --> 00:04:03.983
This is, without a doubt, due to the fact
that it’s both open to Americans and, crucially,

00:04:03.983 --> 00:04:06.133
a leisure destination.

00:04:06.133 --> 00:04:10.373
Airline demand is traditionally split into
leisure and business, and these two segments

00:04:10.373 --> 00:04:13.703
have had very different stories of recovery.

00:04:13.703 --> 00:04:19.154
Leisure demand picked up quickly and sharply
as soon as stay-at-home orders lifted, while

00:04:19.154 --> 00:04:22.274
business demand has barely increased at all.

00:04:22.274 --> 00:04:27.194
This is no surprise considering that, overwhelmingly,
offices are still closed, conferences are

00:04:27.194 --> 00:04:30.394
still cancelled, and clients are still cautious.

00:04:30.394 --> 00:04:34.254
Therefore, airlines have had to respond by
removing capacity from where people travel

00:04:34.254 --> 00:04:39.174
for work, and adding it to where people travel
for vacation, such as Cabo.

00:04:39.174 --> 00:04:44.514
Right now, there’s a huge amount of capacity
being dumped into the Florida and Mexico markets.

00:04:44.514 --> 00:04:48.894
For example, in the coming winter, there are
twenty-four flights a week scheduled between

00:04:48.894 --> 00:04:52.954
New York and Cabo, versus just two a week
last winter.

00:04:52.954 --> 00:04:57.764
Some leisure destinations have not only recovered
faster, but have come out ahead versus how

00:04:57.764 --> 00:04:59.144
they were before.

00:04:59.144 --> 00:05:03.424
This is certainly the case with Cabo, and
the October 8th flight from Phoenix represented

00:05:03.424 --> 00:05:07.954
the first of many, with Southwest adding this
city-pair to their network.

00:05:07.954 --> 00:05:12.964
Overall, leisure is winning, business is losing,
and therefore airlines that have historically

00:05:12.964 --> 00:05:18.184
been more leisure oriented, like Southwest,
have fared far better than their more business-oriented

00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:19.964
competitors.

00:05:19.964 --> 00:05:24.174
One of those more business-oriented competitors
is United, which announced this new route

00:05:24.174 --> 00:05:27.214
from Milwaukee, Wisconsin to Fort Myers, Florida.

00:05:27.214 --> 00:05:32.344
Much like Southwest's flight, this probably
doesn’t seem that strange but, for United,

00:05:32.344 --> 00:05:35.754
it’s a dramatic deviation from the mean.

00:05:35.754 --> 00:05:41.334
You see, like most legacy carriers, United
has traditionally been a strict follower of

00:05:41.334 --> 00:05:44.314
the hub-and-spoke school of network planning.

00:05:44.314 --> 00:05:49.714
Every single flight they fly, with very limited
exception, either originates or terminates

00:05:49.714 --> 00:05:55.894
in DC, Newark, Chicago, Houston, Denver, Los
Angeles, San Francisco, or Guam—their hub

00:05:55.894 --> 00:05:57.184
airports.

00:05:57.184 --> 00:06:01.854
This flight, though, along with a whole package
of others announced at the same time, does

00:06:01.854 --> 00:06:02.854
not.

00:06:02.854 --> 00:06:08.474
United wouldn’t break from precedent without
a good reason, and so this must mean something.

00:06:08.474 --> 00:06:14.124
Most likely, it indicates a mix of desperation
and innovation—desperation because they’re

00:06:14.124 --> 00:06:18.604
now seriously competing on leisure-routes,
something they avoided before; and innovation

00:06:18.604 --> 00:06:24.654
because United is reinventing its network
design and getting a little more creative.

00:06:24.654 --> 00:06:29.434
Creativity and innovation is what pushes an
industry forward and so, even if it comes

00:06:29.434 --> 00:06:34.654
in the form of a route that is just ever so
slightly different, it demonstrates a characteristic

00:06:34.654 --> 00:06:38.964
that would be welcome once the downturn is
over.

00:06:38.964 --> 00:06:42.924
Airlines are now willing to find revenue wherever
it might be, whereas in the past they might

00:06:42.924 --> 00:06:48.864
have only chosen to pursue the most profitable
form of revenue, so this flight, UA2861, from

00:06:48.864 --> 00:06:54.324
Guam to Singapore on a 787, is another that
could never have existed a year ago.

00:06:54.324 --> 00:06:59.564
Now, Guam is a United hub, but it’s never
had flights to Singapore, definitively never

00:06:59.564 --> 00:07:06.075
had these on a 787, and absolutely never operated
them without passengers and yet now, four

00:07:06.075 --> 00:07:07.934
times a week, they are.

00:07:07.934 --> 00:07:11.734
With the current lack in long-haul flights,
which traditionally carry freight in their

00:07:11.734 --> 00:07:16.445
belly-holds, global cargo capacity is down,
meaning prices are up, meaning airlines can

00:07:16.445 --> 00:07:21.744
reliably turn a slight profit flying passenger
airplanes without passengers.

00:07:21.744 --> 00:07:26.894
In a matter of months, United has turned its
Guam hub essentially into a mini cargo hub,

00:07:26.894 --> 00:07:31.524
with regular flights stopping over from the
US, going on to Asian destinations.

00:07:31.524 --> 00:07:37.294
Beyond this, globally, United now operates
hundreds of cargo-only flights per week, even

00:07:37.294 --> 00:07:40.324
though one year ago, it didn’t operate a
single one.

00:07:40.324 --> 00:07:45.384
Whether it’ll turn Guam into a major passenger
hub for the US to Asia market is an unanswerable

00:07:45.384 --> 00:07:50.064
question right now, but at the very least,
this further demonstrates how airlines will

00:07:50.064 --> 00:07:54.574
truly take any revenue they can get, even
if it involves turning into a cargo airline

00:07:54.574 --> 00:07:56.974
in a matter of months.

00:07:56.974 --> 00:08:01.904
Revenue is, of course, half of the equation
for profitability, but the other part is,

00:08:01.904 --> 00:08:03.784
of course, costs.

00:08:03.784 --> 00:08:09.155
Therefore, for weathering this crisis, airlines
need to get as much revenue as possible, while

00:08:09.155 --> 00:08:11.204
also minimizing cost.

00:08:11.204 --> 00:08:16.334
This American Airlines flight from Eagle County
Airport to Aspen was not about bringing in

00:08:16.334 --> 00:08:18.634
revenue, but rather about reducing costs.

00:08:18.634 --> 00:08:25.054
You see, only 29 miles or 46 kilometers separate
these two airports, meaning nobody’s really

00:08:25.054 --> 00:08:27.074
looking to fly between them.

00:08:27.074 --> 00:08:31.444
However, this flight was part of an overall
flight that went from Dallas, to Eagle County,

00:08:31.444 --> 00:08:35.554
to Aspen, to Montrose, and back to Dallas,
meaning a passenger going to Montrose would

00:08:35.554 --> 00:08:38.214
have to sit through at least two stops.

00:08:38.214 --> 00:08:44.114
Now, a passenger is much more likely to take
a one-stop itinerary than a two, so in a time

00:08:44.114 --> 00:08:49.134
when American Airlines is fighting for every
customer it can get, why would it fly such

00:08:49.134 --> 00:08:51.794
a clearly uncompetitive itinerary?

00:08:51.794 --> 00:08:56.163
Well, the reason is that American didn’t
really care how many people took this flight

00:08:56.163 --> 00:08:59.114
because it was always going to be very close
to none.

00:08:59.114 --> 00:09:04.254
They couldn’t, however, just cancel it because
airlines in the US received a package of financial

00:09:04.254 --> 00:09:08.644
aid from the government in exchange for, among
other things, agreeing to continue serving

00:09:08.644 --> 00:09:11.524
every airport they did previously.

00:09:11.524 --> 00:09:15.994
American had previously served these three
airports which primary act as entry points

00:09:15.994 --> 00:09:20.114
for tourist towns—each of which implemented
restrictions preventing visitors during their

00:09:20.114 --> 00:09:21.654
stay-at-home periods.

00:09:21.654 --> 00:09:25.774
This meant demand for travelling to these
places was essentially zero—in the case

00:09:25.774 --> 00:09:30.644
of Aspen in May, for example, just 7% of American’s
seats were filled.

00:09:30.644 --> 00:09:35.324
Therefore, instead of operating three different
empty flights, they rather operated just one

00:09:35.324 --> 00:09:39.864
that stopped at each of the three airports,
thereby creating that 29-mile flight which

00:09:39.864 --> 00:09:43.284
was, at the time, the shortest commercial
flight in America.

00:09:43.284 --> 00:09:48.334
Until the CARES act’s expired on October
1st, American and other airlines found ways

00:09:48.334 --> 00:09:52.534
to both stay loyal to their new masters in
the government, while also minimizing cost

00:09:52.534 --> 00:09:53.914
to the lowest level.

00:09:53.914 --> 00:09:59.914
But back on the revenue side, on September
25th, Qantas operated QF1570 from Canberra

00:09:59.914 --> 00:10:02.554
to Gold Coast Airport in Australia.

00:10:02.554 --> 00:10:07.314
Exactly one week earlier, on September 18th,
Queensland, the state that the Gold Coast

00:10:07.314 --> 00:10:11.814
is in, announced it was opening its borders
to the Australian Capital Territory, the territory

00:10:11.814 --> 00:10:13.084
that Canberra is in.

00:10:13.084 --> 00:10:17.474
That very same day, Qantas announced that
it would start a new route between these two

00:10:17.474 --> 00:10:19.924
airports one week later.

00:10:19.924 --> 00:10:26.094
Now, normally, when airlines announce new
routes, they do so months or even years in

00:10:26.094 --> 00:10:27.094
advance.

00:10:27.094 --> 00:10:31.124
For example, when Qantas announced a new route
from Brisbane to Chicago, originally slated

00:10:31.124 --> 00:10:37.024
to start in April, 2020 but now pushed back,
they did so in July, 2019—a full eight months

00:10:37.024 --> 00:10:38.384
in advance.

00:10:38.384 --> 00:10:43.314
To shorten this to one week is unprecedented,
but with borders opening and closing at a

00:10:43.314 --> 00:10:46.954
moment’s notice, predicting demand has become
incredibly tough.

00:10:46.954 --> 00:10:52.574
So, in this case, Qantas saw that demand for
travel to Queensland was going to surge, and

00:10:52.574 --> 00:10:57.564
they were able to respond lightning-fast—allowing
for them to capture it.

00:10:57.564 --> 00:11:01.994
Other airlines around the world have done
the same, and the industry, in general, has

00:11:01.994 --> 00:11:04.874
become more agile and data-driven.

00:11:04.874 --> 00:11:09.294
These will be useful skills for airlines to
maintain long into the future as, when there

00:11:09.294 --> 00:11:14.154
is demand, a company’s excuse should never
be that they couldn’t act fast enough to

00:11:14.154 --> 00:11:15.264
capture it.

00:11:15.264 --> 00:11:19.164
Sometimes, though, it’s strategic to ignore
demand.

00:11:19.164 --> 00:11:24.994
On October 6th, American Airlines flew its
last flight from Greenville, North Carolina,

00:11:24.994 --> 00:11:27.964
for at least a month, but likely longer.

00:11:27.964 --> 00:11:32.694
An airline pulling service from a small, regional
airport in a time of economic crisis is nothing

00:11:32.694 --> 00:11:36.364
unexpected, but this case stands out.

00:11:36.364 --> 00:11:41.674
The cut was originally announced as only for
a month, from October 7th to November 4th—a

00:11:41.674 --> 00:11:45.594
date recognizable for its proximity to the
November 3rd US election.

00:11:45.594 --> 00:11:50.174
There’s no reason for an airline to believe
that the economic conditions that make a route

00:11:50.174 --> 00:11:55.124
unprofitable on October 7th would be resolved
by November 4th—if anything, they should

00:11:55.124 --> 00:12:00.604
get worse as winter nears—so suspending
a route for only one month to save money doesn’t

00:12:00.604 --> 00:12:01.634
make sense.

00:12:01.634 --> 00:12:07.284
In addition, this service suspension was announced
along with fourteen others impacting fourteen

00:12:07.284 --> 00:12:12.913
small airports in fourteen different states—including
Pennsylvania, Iowa, North Carolina, and other

00:12:12.913 --> 00:12:15.514
swing states in the 2020 election.

00:12:15.514 --> 00:12:20.464
Also, many of the states that American Airlines
cut service to have incumbent senators who

00:12:20.464 --> 00:12:24.364
polling data indicate might lose on November
3rd.

00:12:24.364 --> 00:12:28.734
This includes Thom Tillis who, as happens
when small airports lose their only commercial

00:12:28.734 --> 00:12:34.533
service, immediately faced pressure from his
constituents to bring flights back to Greenville.

00:12:34.533 --> 00:12:38.514
American Airlines is essentially playing chicken
with the US government.

00:12:38.514 --> 00:12:42.934
With CARES act funding having now expired,
airlines desperately want more financial aid

00:12:42.934 --> 00:12:47.814
from Congress, and with fifteen routes to
fifteen states cut, there are now 30 senators

00:12:47.814 --> 00:12:51.913
that have to answer for why they haven’t
passed an airline relief bill to help save

00:12:51.913 --> 00:12:54.244
these small towns’ service.

00:12:54.244 --> 00:12:58.434
In the end, three of these suspensions didn’t
go through, but most others were extended

00:12:58.434 --> 00:13:02.304
another month—thereby ratcheting up the
pressure on Congress by signaling that these

00:13:02.304 --> 00:13:07.444
temporary suspensions might not be so temporary
if they don’t get the relief they say they

00:13:07.444 --> 00:13:08.444
need.

00:13:08.444 --> 00:13:12.033
While in the US and elsewhere, airlines’
continued survival is increasingly linked

00:13:12.033 --> 00:13:15.434
to politics, in other places, it’s always
been.

00:13:15.434 --> 00:13:20.354
China’s aviation recovery has played a key
part in the country’s narrative of overall

00:13:20.354 --> 00:13:24.614
recovery which is, at least compared to other
places, genuine.

00:13:24.614 --> 00:13:30.154
On August 28th, Chengdu Airlines flight 2712
was the first to leave from the airport that,

00:13:30.154 --> 00:13:34.663
eight months earlier, undoubtably played a
central role in spreading the virus across

00:13:34.663 --> 00:13:37.964
the globe—Wuhan Tianhe International.

00:13:37.964 --> 00:13:43.244
This flight, though, was the first of hundreds
to leave Wuhan that day and that day, in fact,

00:13:43.244 --> 00:13:48.124
was the first when the number of flights in
the country reached 100% relative to January

00:13:48.124 --> 00:13:49.694
1st of the same year.

00:13:49.694 --> 00:13:56.054
China’s aviation recovery was fast, in part
because China’s virus recovery was fast.

00:13:56.054 --> 00:13:59.854
The country’s airlines are not completely
back to normal yet—their load factors and

00:13:59.854 --> 00:14:04.663
fares are lower than before and their international
market is still nearly nonexistent—but China’s

00:14:04.663 --> 00:14:08.874
airlines, along with those of New Zealand,
Australia, and all the other countries that

00:14:08.874 --> 00:14:14.033
have successfully curbed the virus, demonstrate
that the economic crisis cannot end until

00:14:14.033 --> 00:14:19.033
the public health crisis does and therefore,
no matter which one cares about more, the

00:14:19.033 --> 00:14:24.154
route to recovery always starts with health.

00:14:24.154 --> 00:14:29.434
The YouTube algorithm, as any creator knows,
is optimized for one thing—keeping the average

00:14:29.434 --> 00:14:34.624
person on the platform for the longest time—but
for us in the educational niche, our audience

00:14:34.624 --> 00:14:37.524
likes different things than the average YouTube
viewer.

00:14:37.524 --> 00:14:41.484
We know what our audience wants, but sometimes
that doesn’t line up with the stuff that

00:14:41.484 --> 00:14:42.763
the YouTube algorithm will favor.

00:14:42.763 --> 00:14:47.734
For example, I wanted to make this video longer,
but I had to keep it within the key ten to

00:14:47.734 --> 00:14:51.084
fifteen minute length so that performance
didn’t tank.

00:14:51.084 --> 00:14:55.474
Instead, I’ve cut it down from covering
ten flights to just eight, and put an extended

00:14:55.474 --> 00:15:00.004
version with what I thought were the least
important of the ten on Nebula.

00:15:00.004 --> 00:15:05.254
This is just one of many pieces of exclusive
content you can get on there, including Showmakers—the

